   "chosen": "Vite 8 + React 19 SPA, served by a thin Hono/Node API server",
   "rejected": "Next.js 16",
   "rest": [
    "Not because Vite is \"lighter\" as a reflex — because three of this tool's actual named pressures point the same direction and Next.js's headline advantage (RSC reading the filesystem with no API layer) turns out not to survive contact with this app's other requirements:",
    "The one thing Next.js would have made marginally easier — not re-inventing a REST layer for the initial tree/file read — is not worth inheriting App Router conventions, RSC/client component boundary management, and a heavier dev server for a UI that is fundamentally an interactive SPA (live search-as- you-type, filterable tree, clickable cross-references) with no SEO, no scale, and no hosting concern to justify SSR. **Confidence: HIGH** — this reasoning is about this app's specific shape, not a general Next-vs-Vite verdict; a networked, multi-user, or SEO-relevant Labelore would tip the other way."
   ],
   "reasons": [
    {
     "lead": "A CLI-launched path argument is the primary entry point, every single time it runs.",
     "body": "This tool is relaunched dozens of times against different `.planning/` directories, not deployed once and left running. Next.js's idiomatic ways to receive that argument are an env var read at the top of Server Components (clean, but then a *separate* CLI wrapper script has to parse argv and re-exec `next dev` with the env var set) or a custom server (`next()` wrapping a Node `http.Server`) — which Next's own docs now explicitly discourage: \"should only be used when the integrated router of Next.js can't meet your app requirements,\" and Vercel no longer ships an App Router custom-server example. Vite's equivalent is: you already own the Node entrypoint (it's your Hono app), so `process.argv[2]` is read in the same file that starts the server. No indirection, no discouraged pattern."
    },
    {
     "lead": "RSC's fs-read advantage is real but narrow, and this app needs a request/response and streaming layer regardless.",
     "body": "Full-text search (a named v1 requirement) needs a query endpoint. Cross-reference click-through needs a way to fetch a specific artifact on demand. The v2 file watcher (must not be precluded) needs a long-lived stream to the browser. All three need an API surface whether or not the *initial* page load can skip one via RSC. Once that surface exists, RSC only saves you the handlers for the first paint — and Server Actions (Next's other \"skip the API layer\" mechanism) are request/response RPC, not a good fit for the SSE stream the watcher needs, which pushes you back toward Route Handlers anyway. A plain Hono server gives you REST + SSE in one uniform, un-clever mechanism from day one."
    },
    {
     "lead": "Dev-loop speed and boot time favor Vite concretely, not just by reputation.",
     "body": "Next 16 with Turbopack is fast for what it is, but it still carries file-system route discovery, RSC module graph compilation, and a `.next` cache that a tool relaunched constantly against different target paths doesn't benefit from. Vite's dev server (esbuild-based dependency pre-bundling, native ESM in the browser) has near-instant cold start and HMR for an app this size, and there is no production build step required to iterate — you run `vite dev` behind the same Hono process that will later serve `vite build` output, so \"clone and run\" stays literally that."
    }
   ]
